- **Platforms:** Newsletter (Growth Memo, 25,000+ subscribers), Podcast (Contrarian Marketing, co-hosted with Eli Schwartz), LinkedIn, Search Engine Journal column

---
## Why This Expert
Kevin Indig is arguably the strongest single fit for "AI-powered SEO content production" in B2B SaaS today. He operated growth and SEO functions inside three major SaaS/e-commerce organizations (Shopify, G2, Atlassian) before going independent, which means his frameworks come from operating budgets and real product roadmaps, not just outside observation.

What separates him from most commentators is his research cadence: Growth Memo publishes near-weekly, data-backed studies — clickstream analyses, citation audits across hundreds of thousands of AI conversations, and structured "Growth Intelligence Briefs" that track what is actually changing week to week in Google and LLM behavior. He's built and published original frameworks like the "decoupling of clicks from impact," which argues that AI search visibility now drives brand consideration and revenue in ways traditional click-based attribution can't capture — directly relevant to how B2B SaaS teams should measure content ROI in 2026.

His advisory client list (Ramp, Dropbox, Nextdoor, Snapchat) and continued syndication on Search Engine Journal indicate he's trusted by both practitioners and larger media outlets, giving him credibility as both an operator and an educator.

---
## Recent Content

### Piece 1
- **Title:** "The science of how AI pays attention"
- **Date:** February 16, 2026
- **Link:** https://www.growth-memo.com/p/the-science-of-how-ai-pays-attention
- **Summary:** Indig analyzed 1.2 million search results to understand how large language models actually process and select source content, concluding that AI systems behave more like a rushed editor skimming for usable facts than a careful reader working through a full document.
- **Key Takeaways:**
  - AI systems prioritize front-loaded, clearly structured information over comprehensive "ultimate guide" formats.
  - Content structured for fast extraction (clear entities, definitive statements) outperforms exhaustive coverage.
  - The findings challenge legacy SEO content advice built around depth and word count as ranking signals.
- **Notable Quotes:** Indig describes the AI reading behavior he found as that of <cite index="46-1">"a busy editor, not a patient student."</cite>
- **Practical Applications:** B2B SaaS content teams should restructure cornerstone pages so the highest-value facts (pricing, differentiators, use cases) appear in the first 500–600 words, rather than burying them under introductory context.

### Piece 2
- **Title:** "Growth Intelligence Brief #18"
- **Date:** May 15, 2026
- **Link:** https://www.growth-memo.com/p/growth-intelligence-brief-18
- **Summary:** A weekly research brief covering Microsoft's public explanation of how Bing's index now functions as a "grounding" layer for AI answers rather than a traditional ranked list, plus practical guidance on auditing whether AI crawlers can actually read a site's key facts.
- **Key Takeaways:**
  - <cite index="47-2">Microsoft's framing treats the goal as fetching the best information to synthesize into a reliable, verifiable answer rather than just fetching the best documents.</cite>
  - Factual fidelity — whether an indexed version of a page preserves the original meaning — is becoming a core quality metric for AI systems.
  - Freshness failures carry a higher cost when content is used to ground answers rather than simply surfaced as an option.
- **Practical Applications:** Run a crawler-access audit (e.g., using Firecrawl or server logs) on top commercial pages to confirm AI crawlers can retrieve pricing, feature, and comparison facts that may be hidden behind JavaScript rendering.

---
## Overall Themes
Indig's recurring throughline is that classic SEO metrics (rankings, clicks, traffic) are decoupling from business impact, and that organic growth teams need new measurement systems built around AI visibility, citation frequency, and brand consideration rather than legacy rank tracking. His frameworks consistently push teams to: (1) treat AI crawlers and classic search crawlers as needing different technical strategies, (2) restructure content for fact-dense, front-loaded extraction rather than narrative-style long-form, (3) invest in original data and proprietary research as the most defensible way to earn AI citations, and (4) build measurement systems (like his Growth Intelligence Briefs) that track topic-level and vertical-level shifts rather than single keyword rankings.

